---
// /agents/: what AGENTS.md does, its rules in plain language, and each course's scope.
// Paraphrases dight-refactor/starter-kits/src/agents/ (base.md, scope-250.md, scope-340.md).
// Keep it in step with those files when they change.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Badge from '../../components/Badge.astro';
import PageMeta from '../../components/PageMeta.astro';
---

<Base
  title="AGENTS.md, explained"
  description="What the AGENTS.md file in your repo tells the AI agent, in plain language."
>
  <PageMeta courses={['dight250', 'dight340']} />
  <h1>AGENTS.md, explained</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    The starter kit put a file called <code>AGENTS.md</code> at the top of your repo. I wrote it. Every AI agent that
    works in your repo reads it first, so it sets the rules for how the agent helps you.
  </p>

  <p>
    Copilot and Claude Code both read <code>AGENTS.md</code> on their own; you don’t have to paste it or point to it.
    Both courses share the same rules, plus a short section on what each course covers.
  </p>

  <h2>What it tells the agent</h2>

  <h3>Who it’s helping</h3>
  <p>
    A student who is here to learn, not just to ship code. Anything it writes, you have to be able to read, change, and
    explain, so it should write code you can explain and help you understand it.
  </p>

  <h3>Before it writes code</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>If you haven’t said what you’re building and who it’s for, it asks.</li>
    <li>It proposes a short plan (which files, what approach) and waits for you to agree.</li>
    <li>It keeps each change small enough for you to review in one sitting.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>When it writes code</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Accessible by default: landmarks, headings in order, labels on every form control, useful <code>alt</code> text,
      enough contrast, no meaning carried by colour alone, everything usable from the keyboard, and focus outlines
      never removed.
    </li>
    <li>
      Course conventions: <code>const</code> and <code>let</code>, <code>===</code>, scripts loaded with
      <code>defer</code>, and relative paths so pages work on GitHub Pages.
    </li>
    <li>It explains each choice in plain language.</li>
    <li>It links the MDN page for everything it used. MDN is the course’s reference, not blogs or AI summaries.</li>
    <li>It points out one thing worth digging into, for your NOTES.md, but doesn’t write that section.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Never</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Write your reflections, or draft, outline, or rewrite them.</li>
    <li>
      Write any section of your <a href="/notes/"><code>NOTES.md</code></a>. If you ask, it offers questions to
      help you write them instead. It can list the prompts you used, but you write the AI use section.
    </li>
    <li>Add a library, framework, package, or CDN link without asking, or ever one outside the course.</li>
    <li>Change <code>AGENTS.md</code>, <code>.github/</code>, or <code>.claude/</code>, unless you tell it the instructor asked for the change.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What each course covers</h2>
  <p>
    The scope covers the whole course, not just this week. The agent can always <em>explain</em> something we haven’t
    reached yet. If you ask it to <em>write</em> something outside the scope, it says so, explains why, and offers a way
    that fits.
  </p>

  <section aria-labelledby="scope-250">
    <h3 id="scope-250"><Badge kind="250" /> HTML and CSS only</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        No JavaScript, except in the Dec 9 bridge Spark. If something seems to need JS, the agent looks for the HTML or
        CSS way.
      </li>
      <li>No frameworks or preprocessors: no Tailwind, Bootstrap, or Sass. Hand-written CSS in <code>.css</code> files.</li>
      <li>Web fonts are fine.</li>
      <li>Valid HTML from the start, checked with the W3C validator.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="scope-340">
    <h3 id="scope-340"><Badge kind="340" /> Vanilla JavaScript, Svelte 5, and Astro</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>No React, Vue, jQuery, Tailwind, or other UI or CSS frameworks.</li>
      <li>Svelte 5 runes only (<code>$state</code>, <code>$derived</code>, <code>$props</code>…), not Svelte 4 syntax.</li>
      <li>
        TypeScript only where Svelte or Astro uses it, and the agent explains every annotation. You learn to read it,
        not write it from scratch.
      </li>
      <li>
        It proposes every <code>npm install</code> and waits for you. Vite, Svelte, Astro, Vitest, ESLint, Playwright,
        and axe are approved; anything else, check with me first.
      </li>
      <li>No console errors, and visible, announced loading and error states for any data it fetches.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <details>
    <summary>Predict: you ask the agent to “just add Tailwind.” What does it do?</summary>
    <p>
      It doesn’t add it. Tailwind is outside the scope in both courses, so the agent says so, explains why it’s out of
      scope for this course, and offers a way to get the same result with what the course does use: hand-written CSS
      in 250, or the 340 stack. Even for an in-scope library, it would ask before adding anything.
    </p>
  </details>

  <h2>Don’t edit it</h2>
  <p>
    <code>AGENTS.md</code> is the same for everyone in the course. If a rule gets in your way, ask me rather than
    changing it. In DIGHT 340, also don’t add a <code>CLAUDE.md</code>: Claude Code reads a <code>CLAUDE.md</code>
    <em>instead of</em> <code>AGENTS.md</code>, so the course rules would stop loading.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
  }

  section h3 {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
  }
</style>
